WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.895
A rider opens the app.

2
00:00:01.915 --> 00:00:04.055
The price must be fresh, now.

3
00:00:04.075 --> 00:00:06.030
A batch job each hour is too slow.

4
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:07.590
You need a stream.

5
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:12.490
Three ideas: the Kafka log, delivery guarantees, and event time.

6
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.575
First: Kafka is a log.

7
00:00:14.595 --> 00:00:16.750
Producers append messages at the end.

8
00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.260
Consumers read at their own speed.

9
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.360
A topic is split into partitions.

10
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.445
That is how Kafka scales.

11
00:00:23.465 --> 00:00:26.133
Order holds only inside one partition.

12
00:00:26.153 --> 00:00:28.855
So the key decides what stays in order.

13
00:00:28.875 --> 00:00:31.043
Each consumer remembers its position.

14
00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:33.905
That position is the offset.

15
00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:37.065
Second: can a message be lost, or come twice?

16
00:00:37.085 --> 00:00:38.990
At most once can lose a message.

17
00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.728
At least once can repeat one.

18
00:00:40.748 --> 00:00:42.925
Exactly once costs more.

19
00:00:42.945 --> 00:00:47.425
Most systems pick at least once, and make the write idempotent.

20
00:00:47.445 --> 00:00:50.410
Third: events arrive late, and out of order.

21
00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.135
A phone goes into a tunnel.

22
00:00:52.155 --> 00:00:54.545
Its events arrive ten minutes later.

23
00:00:54.565 --> 00:00:56.457
So use the time the event happened.

24
00:00:56.477 --> 00:00:58.305
Not the time it arrived.

25
00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:02.780
A watermark says: I think I have seen everything up to this time.

26
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.415
Last: a stream job remembers things.

27
00:01:05.435 --> 00:01:07.078
A count per window is state.

28
00:01:07.098 --> 00:01:09.725
Flink saves that state in checkpoints.

29
00:01:09.745 --> 00:01:13.900
After a crash, the job restarts from the last checkpoint.

30
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.810
Quick recap of streaming.

31
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.335
Order is per partition.

32
00:01:17.355 --> 00:01:19.485
At least once, with idempotent writes.

33
00:01:19.505 --> 00:01:21.270
And use event time.

34
00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.390
Now start the first lesson below.
